<p>In 2000, a team in Udine described a man who had had a stroke in his left frontal lobe and anterior cingulate. He was a Friulian-Italian bilingual. He had no aphasia in the ordinary sense: his grammar held, his words came, his comprehension was intact. What he had lost was the ability to stay in one language.</p>

<p>Asked to speak Italian, he produced <strong>58.3% of his utterances in Italian, 40.6% in Friulian</strong>, and 1.1% that mixed the two. Asked to speak Friulian, he produced 56.5% in Friulian and 43.5% in Italian. He knew what he was supposed to be doing. He apologised, repeatedly, immediately after each lapse. He also switched into Friulian when addressing hospital staff in Trieste who did not speak a word of it, which is the detail that makes the case strange: the sociolinguistic rule that governs normal code-switching - only switch to a language your listener shares - had survived in his knowledge and abandoned his behaviour.</p>

<p>That dissociation is the reason this article exists. Bilingual aphasia is the only natural experiment in which the languages inside one head are taken apart by something other than a syllabus, and the way they come apart tells you things about your own vocabulary that no amount of self-testing will.</p>

<h2>What the Cases Are, and What They Are Not</h2>

<p>Aphasia is acquired language impairment after brain injury, most often stroke. In a bilingual it can affect both languages equally, affect them unequally, or - in the rarest cases - erase one and leave the other standing. Around <strong>60%</strong> of reported cases show broadly comparable impairment in both languages. The rest do not, and the minority is where the information is.</p>

<p>Before going further, one honest warning about the evidence. This literature is dominated by single case reports, which are published precisely <em>because</em> they are unusual, and premorbid proficiency in each language can only be estimated by self-rating - which Tomoschuk, Ferreira and Gollan showed in 2018 does not reliably match objective measurement. Paradis' own 2001 review of 132 cases drew <strong>99 of them (75%) from just two group studies</strong>, both of closely related language pairs (Catalan-Spanish and Galician-Spanish). Read the percentages below as a map of what has been reported, not as an actuarial table.</p>

<h2>Paradis' Six Patterns</h2>

<p>Michel Paradis, who spent a career on this, sorted the outcomes into six patterns that are still the standard vocabulary.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Parallel recovery</strong> - both languages impaired and restored at roughly the same rate, relative to their premorbid levels. The common case.</li>
<li><strong>Differential recovery</strong> - one language recovers better than the other, relative to how well it was known before.</li>
<li><strong>Blended recovery</strong> - the patient systematically mixes features of both languages at every level, phonology through syntax.</li>
<li><strong>Selective recovery</strong> - one or more languages simply do not come back.</li>
<li><strong>Successive recovery</strong> - one language does not begin to return until another has recovered as far as it will go.</li>
<li><strong>Pathological switching and mixing</strong> - involuntary alternation, either between utterances or inside them.</li>
</ul>

<p>In Paradis' 2001 review of 132 cases published between 1990 and 1999, the split was <strong>61% parallel (81 cases), 18% differential (24), 9% blended (12), 7% selective (9) and 5% successive (6)</strong>.</p>

<p>Fabbro's 2001 study is the cleanest single dataset: 20 early Friulian-Italian bilinguals, all assessed with the Bilingual Aphasia Test rather than ad hoc tasks. Thirteen (65%) showed comparable impairment in both languages, four (20%) were significantly worse in L2, three (15%) significantly worse in L1 - and of those three, one comparison sat at p = 0.07, which is a reminder of how thin these subgroup findings are at n = 20.</p>

<h2>Two Old Laws That Cannot Both Be Right</h2>

<p>Two nineteenth-century rules have framed the question ever since, and they make opposite predictions.</p>

<p><strong>Ribot's law</strong> (1882) says that earlier-formed memories are more resistant to brain damage. Applied to language: your first language should survive best, because it was laid down first and rehearsed most.</p>

<p><strong>Pitres' law</strong> (1895) says the language that was <em>dominant</em> before the injury recovers first, regardless of when it was learned. A heritage speaker who grew up speaking one language at home and then lived twenty years in another should, on this account, recover the second one better.</p>

<p>Albert and Obler reviewed <strong>108 cases</strong> in 1978 expecting to see one law win. They found comparable distributions of parallel and non-parallel impairment in early "compound" bilinguals and in late learners alike, with age, age of acquisition and education all pulling on the outcome. Neither law is a law. What the field has instead is a set of four variables that consistently predict how badly each language is hit: <strong>age of acquisition, premorbid proficiency, current use and exposure, and linguistic similarity</strong> between the languages.</p>

<h2>What the Numbers Actually Say</h2>

<p>The most useful recent synthesis is Kuzmina, Goral, Norvik and Weekes' 2019 meta-analysis: <strong>65 studies, 130 cases</strong>, coded for age of acquisition, proficiency, use, and how similar the two languages were. Three findings matter for anyone who learns languages on purpose.</p>

<p><strong>First, the first-learned language has an advantage, and it is modest.</strong> Across the trimmed sample, overall performance in L1 was about <strong>10% better than in L2</strong> (risk ratio 1.10). The gap was <strong>6% for auditory comprehension</strong> and <strong>10% for oral production</strong>. Ten per cent is real and it is not destiny.</p>

<p><strong>Second, the advantage grows with every year of delay.</strong> Age of acquisition was a significant moderator, with the ratio shifting by roughly <strong>1% per year</strong> of later acquisition, and the effect was strongest for oral production. Learning a language at forty is not the same as learning it at six; the research says so plainly rather than politely.</p>

<p><strong>Third, and least intuitive, is what happened with use.</strong> The language being used in the person's environment at the time of the stroke tended to recover better. Several studies found the linguistic environment also shaped response to therapy. This is not a fact about stroke. It is a fact about activation: a language you have stopped using is not sitting in storage at full strength, waiting. It has been quietly deactivated, which is the same process the attrition literature describes in healthy learners.</p>

<h2>The Man Who Switched Into a Language He Barely Spoke</h2>

<p>In 2007 Leemann and colleagues reported a French-speaking patient who, after his stroke, switched to German - a language he had learned at school, barely mastered, and hardly ever used - and stayed there most of the time.</p>

<p>That looks like a paradox until you read the authors' explanation. The patient was not retrieving German better than French. He was using <strong>metalinguistic knowledge</strong> - explicit, consciously learned rules about how the language works - to compensate for losing access to the implicit, procedural system that had once produced French automatically. School German was the most explicitly encoded language he owned. When the automatic route went down, the manual route was all that was left, and it happened to be in German.</p>

<p>This is the single most useful case in the literature for adult learners, because it is your situation in reverse. Adults typically build a language on explicit knowledge first and hope automaticity arrives later. That case shows the two systems can be separated by injury - and, by implication, that they are separate systems. A learner who can explain the past tense and cannot produce it in speech has not yet built the second one.</p>

<h2>Selection Is Its Own Skill</h2>

<p>Go back to the Friulian-Italian man who could not stay in one language. His lesion was frontal, involving the anterior cingulate. He had no aphasia: no anomia, no agrammatism, no comprehension loss. What he had lost was <em>selection</em>.</p>

<p>Pathological mixing and pathological switching are now understood as different failures with different lesion sites. Mixing - two languages inside one utterance - is associated mainly with left parietotemporal damage and is closely tied to word-finding failure: the non-target word is simply more available than the target one, so it comes out. Switching - alternating between utterances, even with a listener who cannot follow - is associated with frontal and cingulate damage, and appears to be a failure of a general control system rather than a language system.</p>

<p>There is also a 2025 interview study of 19 bilingual people with aphasia which found something deflationary and important: most showed no atypical mixing at all, and when they did switch, it usually obeyed the pragmatic context - they switched to a language they believed the interviewer shared. Only three showed marked involuntary switching, and those three reported corresponding difficulty with language control. The dramatic cases are real but they are not the norm, and the older clinical habit of labelling any mixing "pathological" was overreading a strategy as a symptom.</p>

<p>The takeaway is structural. Knowing two languages and being able to move between them on demand are handled by different machinery. That is why <a href="/blog/code-switching-bilingual-science/">code-switching carries measurable switch costs</a> even in fluent speakers, and why people who can read a language beautifully can freeze when asked to speak it.</p>

<h2>The Finding That Should Change How You Review</h2>

<p>Here is the part that matters most for flashcards, and it comes from treatment research rather than from lesion studies.</p>

<p>Swathi Kiran and Chaleece Sandberg ran a naming treatment in <em>one</em> language with 17 bilingual participants and measured two things: whether the trained words improved, and whether anything transferred to the other language. Trained items improved in <strong>14 of 17</strong> participants. Within-language generalisation to semantically related untrained words appeared in <strong>10</strong>. Generalisation to the <em>translations</em> of the trained words in the untrained language appeared in only <strong>5</strong>.</p>

<p>A 2024 meta-analysis by Lee and Faroqi-Shah reached the same place from 40-odd studies: treatment in bilingual aphasia reliably improves trained items, but produces only <strong>marginally small</strong> generalisation both within a language and across to the translation equivalents. Their conclusion was blunt - treat each language separately, because cross-language transfer is not something you can rely on.</p>

<p>Sit with what that means. Two words that mean the same thing, in two languages you speak every day, are not one entry with two labels. Training one improves the other only sometimes, and modestly. The semantic representation is shared - that is why the translation is easy to <em>recognise</em> - but the route from meaning to the specific sound pattern of that specific language is separate, and has to be built separately.</p>

<blockquote>
<p>This is the empirical case against translation-based study. Not that translation is wrong, but that it trains the wrong half of the pair. You can know that <code>la mesa</code> means "the table" and still not be able to say <code>la mesa</code> when you need a table.</p>
</blockquote>

<h2>What a Learner Should Do With This</h2>

<p>None of this is advice about stroke recovery. It is evidence about architecture, and architecture has consequences for how you build.</p>

<h3>1. Count retrieval paths, not known words</h3>

<p>If you can read a word, hear it, and produce it, you have three paths, and they are built by three different activities. A deck card that shows you the foreign word and asks for the meaning trains one of them. If your goal is speaking, the productive direction is not optional, and the research above says the two do not automatically inform each other - which is why <a href="/blog/flashcard-direction-l1-l2-which-way-round/">direction in flashcards is a design decision, not a detail</a>.</p>

<h3>2. Make every new word do something before it becomes a card</h3>

<p>Recognition is cheap and it is the condition the therapy studies found easiest to move. Production is the expensive path. Produce the word once, out loud, in a sentence of your own, before it enters the deck.</p>

<h3>3. Test grammar and vocabulary separately</h3>

<p>The Leemann case is a demonstration that explicit knowledge and procedural skill are dissociable. If you can conjugate a verb on paper and cannot do it while talking, you have the explicit one. Build the other with speed drills, not with more explanation.</p>

<h3>4. Treat switching as a drill, not a symptom</h3>

<p>Bilinguals pay a measurable cost when they change language, and the control system that manages it is trainable in the ordinary sense. Practise: read a paragraph, then summarise it aloud in the other language. Or hold a conversation in which you must answer every third question in your weaker language. It feels artificial because it is artificial, and it targets the exact mechanism that a frontal lesion can knock out.</p>

<h3>5. Schedule maintenance, because deactivation is real</h3>

<p>The finding that the language in use at the time of injury recovers better is the stroke version of a fact you already know from the attrition literature: unused knowledge loses activation before it loses storage. A language you have not touched in a year is not intact and waiting. Give each language a floor - twenty minutes a week of production - rather than letting it lapse and planning to "come back to it".</p>

<h3>6. Learn the register you will actually need</h3>

<p>Premorbid <em>use</em>, not just premorbid proficiency, predicted outcomes. A language you only ever used to order food is, functionally, a language you know how to order food in. If you need to argue with a landlord in it, that path has to exist before you need it.</p>

<h2>The Honest Limits</h2>

<p>Three cautions, because this is a literature that invites overreach.</p>

<p>Case reports are selected for surprise. For every patient who lost one language and kept the other, there are many with boring parallel impairment who were never written up, which means the exotic patterns are overrepresented in exactly the papers that get quoted.</p>

<p>Premorbid proficiency cannot be measured after the fact. Every study here rests on self-report or on retrospective estimation, and self-report is known to be imperfect.</p>

<p>Language similarity confounds almost everything. Catalan and Spanish, Galician and Spanish, Friulian and Italian - these pairs share so much vocabulary that "two languages" is arguably one lexicon with two accents. The findings from those pairs may not transfer to Spanish and Mandarin, and the meta-analysis is candid that its sample is skewed toward the easy pairs.</p>

<h2>What the Cases Are Really Showing</h2>

<p>Put the pieces together and a picture forms that is more useful than any single study.</p>

<p>Meaning is stored once. The words are stored separately, per language, with their own sound patterns and their own routes out. Grammar-as-knowledge and grammar-as-reflex are separate systems, and the second is the one that makes you fluent. The ability to choose among your languages is a third system again, sitting closer to general executive control than to language, which is why it can break on its own.</p>

<p>And all of it decays with disuse, in the specific sense that access gets harder while the knowledge stays put.</p>

<p>The people in these studies did not choose to run this experiment. What they show is that a language is not a file you either have or have lost. It is a set of routes across a network, some of which can be cut while others stay open - which is exactly why the work of learning a language is not collecting words but laying road.</p>

